╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ bipartite_parameter_sharing                                                               │
│ patch_description        │ The previous collective parameter sharing (one parameter for all 8 qubits) was highly     │
│                          │ efficient but potentially under-parametrized for the 28-feature IsingZZ map, which        │
│                          │ couples all possible qubit pairs.                                                         │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This update introduces a bipartite sharing scheme: qubits are divided into two groups of  │
│                          │ four (Group A: 0-3, Group B: 4-7). Each layer of rotations now uses two independent       │
│                          │ parameters (one per group) rather than one. This respects the topology of the hypercube   │
│                          │ entangler, where the first two sets of CZ gates (Matchings 1 and 2) act internally within │
│                          │ these groups, while the third set (Matching 3) provides the necessary communication       │
│                          │ between them.                                                                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This change increases the parameters per block from 4 to 8, bringing the total parameter  │
│                          │ count to 50. This remains highly efficient but doubles the expressive power of the model, │
│                          │ allowing it to better differentiate and process the information from the 28 different     │
│                          │ feature couplings.                                                                        │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0269                                                                                   │
